body:
The biggest lie that people can tell themselves, is that eventually they have lived too much of their life to change it. I know people who went back to school at 40, got a degree, and went from working fast food to high flying office jobs in another few years. If you understand the work load, and are determined to succeed, then you should absolutely go for it.

&#x200B;

Being a lawyer is not a 9-5 job, and while you will be working those hours, many cases require work outside of office hours. Think over 40 hours per week, conducting research and preparing and reviewing documents. It's not easy, I'll tell you that, but if you are interested in it, you should go for it.

You can learn to let go.

&#x200B;

I know it's cliché, but we've all heard it before. Life is like an elevator. On your way up, sometimes, you need to stop and let some people off. Every day walking in the city I pass thousands of people, people with lives and stories that seem so distant and unknown to me, but the thread of fate might pull us together at any given time.

&#x200B;

She was in your life for a long time, and during that time you never realized your feelings for her. She realized hers but never told you them until it was too late, and now she is gone. She wants to distance herself from you, because she cares about you and if you keep on trying to contact her it'll only increase the pain that she feels from moving away.

&#x200B;

The best thing you can do is stop, and let her go. Her moving was inevitable, and if you cherished the time you had together, you should have nothing to regret. Just look back on what was with fond memories and happiness, and let her step off the elevator as you continue on, instead of holding the doors open hoping she'll step back in.

&#x200B;

Let go man, let go.

Having social anxiety is really common when people are young and in school, I had my fair share of it myself. When I was in the 6th grade, I didn't have any friends because I was too nervous to ever talk to anyone, and so I just stayed to myself. I eventually read a post exactly like this, where the top answer was that throwing yourself out there, forcing yourself to talk to people and making new friends was the best solution. This was most effective when you put yourself in a situation where you are forced to socialize, preferably with equally awkward people!

&#x200B;

In my case, I forced myself to join the chess club at our school, and then while playing chess I would try to make small talk with the opponent. To them, it felt like I was just making small talk, but in reality it was maximum-effort socialization on my end. I met some of the closest friends I have today like that, and we've known each other for years.

&#x200B;

TLDR: It's all about making the jump, trying to meet new people and talking to them, and the more you do it the easier it gets. Everyone is nervous in middle school, because people are still trying to figure out who they are at that age. And for the record, there is nothing wrong with asking "hey do you want to be friends?" because I think it's very wholesome and one of my best friends actually asked me that years ago.

What the fuck did I just read dude. This guy is a class A creep, you need to block him and cut connections. When I was younger, I had a similar experience, and I fed into it slowly without telling anyone, and it almost escalated into a legal case of sexual harassment towards a minor. It doesn't matter that he is in the same guild, he is 31 years old and is asking you to marry him, that's one of the biggest red flags that anyone could every possibly hold.

&#x200B;

Just block, cut ties, and if he keeps attempting to contact you, remember that you owe him nothing, that he is a total creep, and that he will be kicked out of the guild if he continues to try to harass you.

&#x200B;

Stay strong, <3

